---

<SimulatorLayout
  branch="anatomy-physiology"
  topic="urinary-system"
  description="Interactive kidney and urinary system simulator: watch blood filtered in a nephron, follow water through reabsorption and into the bladder, and change water intake, salt, blood glucose and blood pressure."
  toc={toc}
  quiz={kidneyQuiz}
  references={references}
>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    <p>
      Every day your kidneys filter about 180 litres of fluid from your blood, more than twice your body weight, and
      return almost all of it. What is left becomes about 1–2 litres of urine carrying away wastes and excess water and
      salt. Watch a nephron at work, follow a single water molecule, and see how your kidneys respond when you are thirsty,
      drink a lot, or have high blood sugar.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <KidneySim slot="sim" />

  <Fragment slot="instructions">
    <ol>
      <li>Press <strong>Start</strong>. On the left is the whole urinary system; on the right, one magnified nephron.</li>
      <li>Watch particles enter the <strong>glomerulus</strong>. Cells and proteins stay in the blood; water, salt, glucose and urea are filtered.</li>
      <li>Follow the ringed <strong>water molecule</strong> and read where it is. Most filtered water is reabsorbed into the blood.</li>
      <li>Change <strong>water drunk</strong>, <strong>salt</strong>, <strong>blood glucose</strong> and <strong>blood pressure</strong>, or use the examples, and watch urine volume, concentration, colour and ADH change.</li>
      <li><strong>Click any structure</strong> to learn about it. Use <strong>Empty the bladder</strong> when it gets full.</li>
    </ol>
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="overview">The urinary system</h2>
  <p>
    The urinary system has four parts. Two <strong>kidneys</strong>, each about the size of a fist, sit against the back
    wall of the abdomen just below the ribs. They make urine, which flows down two <strong>ureters</strong> to the
    <strong>urinary bladder</strong>, a muscular bag that stores it. Urine leaves the body through the
    <strong>urethra</strong>, controlled by ring-shaped sphincter muscles. The kidneys receive about a fifth of all the
    blood pumped by the heart through the renal arteries.
  </p>

  <h2 id="kidney">Inside the kidney</h2>
  <p>
    A kidney has an outer <strong>cortex</strong> and an inner <strong>medulla</strong>, and a funnel-shaped
    <strong>renal pelvis</strong> that collects urine. About a million microscopic <strong>nephrons</strong> do the work.
    Each nephron starts in the cortex with a <strong>glomerulus</strong>, a knot of capillaries inside a cup called
    <strong>Bowman’s capsule</strong>, and continues as a long tubule that dips into the medulla and back before
    draining into a <strong>collecting duct</strong>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="nephron">The nephron, step by step</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Filtration:</strong> blood pressure forces water and small molecules (salts, glucose, amino acids, urea) out of the glomerulus into Bowman’s capsule. Blood cells and large proteins stay in the blood.</li>
    <li><strong>Selective reabsorption:</strong> in the <strong>proximal tubule</strong>, all the glucose and amino acids and about two thirds of the water and salt are taken back into the surrounding capillaries, often by active transport.</li>
    <li><strong>The loop of Henle</strong> builds up a very salty medulla: the descending limb loses water, the ascending limb pumps out salt.</li>
    <li><strong>Secretion:</strong> in the proximal and distal tubules, some substances such as hydrogen ions, potassium and many drugs are actively moved from the blood into the tubule.</li>
    <li><strong>The collecting duct</strong> decides the final amount of water in urine, depending on ADH.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="water">Water balance and ADH</h2>
  <p>
    The brain constantly monitors how concentrated the blood is. When you are short of water, the hypothalamus detects
    it and the pituitary gland releases <strong>antidiuretic hormone (ADH)</strong>. ADH inserts water channels
    (aquaporins) into the collecting duct, so water flows out into the salty medulla and back into the blood: you produce
    a small volume of dark, concentrated urine. When you drink a lot, ADH falls, the ducts stay watertight and you produce
    lots of pale, dilute urine. Human urine can range from about 50 to 1,200 mOsm/kg, but the kidneys must always excrete
    at least about half a litre a day to remove wastes. This is an example of <strong>homeostasis</strong> by negative
    feedback.
  </p>

  <h2 id="regulation">Salt, acid and blood pressure</h2>
  <p>
    The kidneys also control the levels of sodium, potassium and calcium in the blood, and its acidity, by adjusting
    reabsorption and secretion. The hormone <strong>aldosterone</strong> from the adrenal glands increases salt
    reabsorption. Because water follows salt, the kidneys help control blood volume and therefore blood pressure; they
    even release a hormone, renin, when pressure falls. They also make erythropoietin, which stimulates red blood cell
    production, and activate vitamin D.
  </p>

  <h2 id="relevance">Kidneys and health</h2>
  <p>
    Doctors test urine because it reveals what is happening in the blood: glucose in urine can indicate diabetes, protein
    can indicate damaged filters. The filtration rate (GFR) is used to measure kidney function. When kidneys fail, wastes
    and fluid build up, and patients may need <strong>dialysis</strong>, where a machine filters the blood, or a
    <strong>kidney transplant</strong>. A person can live a healthy life with only one working kidney.
  </p>

  <h2 id="terms">Key terms</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt>Filtrate</dt>
    <dd>The fluid filtered from the blood into the nephron.</dd>
    <dt>Reabsorption / secretion</dt>
    <dd>Moving substances from the tubule back into the blood / from the blood into the tubule.</dd>
    <dt>Osmoregulation</dt>
    <dd>Controlling the water and salt balance of the body.</dd>
    <dt>Urea</dt>
    <dd>The main nitrogen-containing waste, made in the liver from broken-down proteins.</dd>
  </dl>

  <h2 id="misconceptions">Common misconceptions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>“Kidneys only remove waste.”</strong> They also control water, salt, acid, blood pressure and red blood cell production.</li>
    <li><strong>“Urine is made in the bladder.”</strong> The bladder only stores it; the kidneys make it.</li>
    <li><strong>“Drinking more water always means healthier kidneys.”</strong> Healthy kidneys adjust to a wide range of intakes; extreme amounts can be harmful.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout callout-warn">
    <p>
      <strong>Educational model only.</strong> This simulator uses simplified textbook values and cannot assess kidney
      function, hydration or blood sugar in a real person. It is not medical advice or clinical guidance. If you have
      concerns about your health, speak to a qualified healthcare professional. See our <a href="/disclaimer/">educational
      and medical disclaimer</a>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="takeaways">Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Kidneys → ureters → bladder → urethra.</li>
    <li>Nephrons filter blood, then reabsorb what the body needs and secrete extra wastes.</li>
    <li>Over 99 % of filtered water is reabsorbed; ADH controls how much in the collecting duct.</li>
    <li>The kidneys maintain homeostasis of water, salts, acidity and blood pressure.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="muted">Model note: whole-body values use simplified textbook relationships. The nephron animation shows the share of each substance reabsorbed in each region; real nephrons have many more transport processes.</p>
</SimulatorLayout>
